add('<h1>Assignment 3: Options, Option Risk, and Portfolio Construction</h1><p class="sub">FinTech 545 · November 2026</p>')
add('<p>All returns are arithmetic. VaR and ES are positive amounts for losses; a negative VaR means the relevant lower-tail quantile is a gain. Predictions below are based on the supplied quotes, positions, and descriptive statistics before model fitting. Sample standard deviations use the usual degrees-of-freedom correction.</p>')
p=D['problem1'];R=p['rows']; flagged=[x for x in R if x['flag']][0]
add('<h2>1. Implied volatility from a dirty chain</h2><h3>Predict</h3>')
add('<p>Theoretical call minus put is S exp(−qT) − K exp(−rT). The table reports market call mid minus put mid minus this value; the comparison band is the sum of the two full quoted spreads. A positive gap means the call is expensive relative to the put. I expected an isolated bad quote to break parity, while a shared movement in call and put would be harder to flag. At a strike where parity holds and both prices have an implied volatility, strict monotonicity of each option price in volatility forces the call and put implied volatilities to agree.</p>')
add(tab(['Strike','Parity gap ($)','Spread band ($)','Flag'],[[f(x['strike'],0),f(x['parity_gap'],3),f(x['band'],3),'Yes' if x['flag'] else ''] for x in R]))
fk=flagged['strike'];nb={x['strike']:x for x in R}
add(f'<p>Only K = {f(fk,0)} is flagged: the {f(flagged["parity_gap"])} gap exceeds the {f(flagged["band"])} band. The gap is positive, so either the call is too expensive or the put is too cheap. Looking at the neighbouring quotes before fitting anything: the 175 put is bid/ask 1.66/1.76, barely above the 170 put at 1.50/1.60, even though a put $5 higher in strike and closer to the money should be worth noticeably more (the 180 put is 4.12/4.29). The 175 call, by contrast, sits smoothly between the 170 and 180 calls. My conclusion is that the 175 put quote is bad (most likely stale), and the 175 call is fine. A static trade would still need executable prices, financing, and settlement checked before calling it an arbitrage.</p>')
add('<p>If parity holds at a strike, C − P equals S exp(−qT) − K exp(−rT), which does not depend on volatility. Both the call and the put are GBSM prices with the same S, K, T, r, and q, and GBSM satisfies parity for any single σ. Because each price is strictly increasing in σ, there is only one σ that matches the call and only one that matches the put, and parity forces them to be the same σ. So the call and put implied volatilities must be equal.</p>')
add('<p>A price error ΔP maps to a vol error of roughly ΔP / vega. I therefore expect the least reliable implied volatilities where vega is small relative to the quoted spread: the far out-of-the-money wings (low-strike puts, high-strike calls) whose prices are a few cents, and the deep in-the-money options (low-strike calls, high-strike puts) whose prices are almost all intrinsic value. In the far call wing the 215, 220, and 225 calls are all quoted at the same 0.05/0.10, which already looks like a minimum-price floor rather than real information.</p>')
add('<h3>Fit</h3><p>Each bid, mid, and ask is inverted separately with the generalized Black–Scholes–Merton formula, using 35 calendar days / 365. A dash means the price lies outside the admissible European price interval (or the upper-bound limit). Specifically, call bids at 140–155 and put bids at 205–225 lie below their discounted intrinsic lower bounds, so no nonnegative volatility can reproduce them. Vega is dollars per unit volatility; “cent effect” is the approximate change in volatility percentage points for a $0.01 price change.</p>')
add(tab(['K','Call bid/mid/ask vol (%)','Put bid/mid/ask vol (%)','Call vega','Put vega','Cent effect C/P (vol pt)'],[[f(x['strike'],0),'/'.join('—' if x['Call_'+z] is None else f(100*x['Call_'+z],1) for z in ['bid','mid','ask']),'/'.join('—' if x['Put_'+z] is None else f(100*x['Put_'+z],1) for z in ['bid','mid','ask']),f(x['Call_vega'],2),f(x['Put_vega'],2),f(x['Call_cent_vol_points'],2)+' / '+f(x['Put_cent_vol_points'],2)] for x in R]))
add(img('problem1_smile.png','Mid implied volatilities and bid–ask implied-volatility ranges.'))
add('<h3>Reconcile</h3>')
add(f'<p>At K = 175 the call–put mid volatility difference is {f(100*(flagged["Call_mid"]-flagged["Put_mid"]),2)} vol points. Dividing the parity gap by local vega gives approximately {f(100*flagged["parity_gap"]/flagged["Call_vega"],2)} points. The first-order conversion agrees to approximation; curvature makes it inexact. The flagged strike is clearly visible on the chart, and the plot confirms which side is wrong: the 175 put mid volatility is {pct(flagged["Put_mid"],1)}, against {pct(nb[170.0]["Put_mid"],1)} at 170 and {pct(nb[180.0]["Put_mid"],1)} at 180, while the 175 call ({pct(flagged["Call_mid"],1)}) lies on the smooth curve. The put, not the call, is the bad quote.</p>')
wing=[x for x in R if x['strike']>=215]
add(f'<p>Above 210, call mid volatility climbs from {pct(wing[0]["Call_mid"])} at 215 to {pct(wing[-1]["Call_mid"])} at 225, while put volatility stays near {pct(wing[-1]["Put_mid"])}. This is a property of these quotes, not a statement about the market. The 215, 220, and 225 calls are all quoted at exactly 0.05/0.10: the price has hit a minimum-tick floor and stops falling as the strike rises, so the model has to raise volatility to keep the price constant. Vega makes this worse: at 225 one cent moves implied volatility by {f(wing[-1]["Call_cent_vol_points"],2)} points, so a 5-cent floor is worth several vol points. The bid and ask vols at 225 ({pct(wing[-1]["Call_bid"],1)} to {pct(wing[-1]["Call_ask"],1)}) show how little the quote pins down. The call–put price gap in this wing stays inside the spread band, so the specified parity flag cannot detect it. Outside the bad strike and upper-call wing, volatility is higher on lower strikes and slopes down toward higher strikes, consistent with relatively strong demand for downside protection.</p>')
p=D['problem2'];add('<h2>2. American options and discrete dividends</h2><h3>Predict</h3>')
add(f'<p>The Week 7 necessary condition compares each dividend with the interest cost of early payment of the strike. The thresholds are ${f(p["thresholds"][0],3)} at 0.10 years and ${f(p["thresholds"][1],3)} at 0.40 years. Thus $0.25 cannot justify early call exercise at the first date; $1.50 can at the second date for sufficiently high stock prices. Dividends should lower both calls and raise both puts. They should make call exercise more attractive before the second dividend and put exercise less attractive because the put benefits from waiting for the price drop.</p>')
