def conv(typ,n):
 return p[typ][str(n)]['american_div'] if str(n) in p[typ] else p[typ]['convergence_large_n'][str(n)]
NS=[100,105,150,155,200,205,300,305]
add(tab(['N','American call with dividends','American put with dividends'],[[n,f(conv('Call',n),5),f(conv('Put',n),5)] for n in NS]))
add(f'<p>The values do not converge monotonically. Neighbouring N give values that jump back and forth (call {f(conv("Call",100),4)} at N = 100 against {f(conv("Call",105),4)} at N = 105, but {f(conv("Call",150),4)} at 150 against {f(conv("Call",155),4)} at 155). The reason is where the strike falls relative to the terminal nodes: as N changes, the node closest to K moves from just above to just below the strike, so the discretized payoff changes and the price oscillates. The size of the swing shrinks as N grows: from N = 200 onward the call varies by under half a cent ({f(conv("Call",300),4)} at 300, {f(conv("Call",305),4)} at 305). Averaging two neighbouring N is a cheap way to cancel most of the oscillation.</p>')
