add(tab(['Option','∂V/∂D₁','∂V/∂D₂'],[[typ+' '+ex,f(p[typ]['dividend_sensitivity'][ex+'_D1'],4),f(p[typ]['dividend_sensitivity'][ex+'_D2'],4)] for typ in ['Call','Put'] for ex in ['european','american']]))
add('<h3>Reconcile</h3>')
add(tab(['Option','No-dividend exercise premium','With-dividend exercise premium'],[[typ,f(p[typ]['200']['american_no_div']-p[typ]['200']['european_no_div'],5),f(p[typ]['200']['american_div']-p[typ]['200']['european_div'],5)] for typ in ['Call','Put']]))
sens=p['Call']['dividend_sensitivity']
add(f'<p>Both predictions held. The call has no early-exercise premium without dividends and a premium of about {f(p["Call"]["200"]["american_div"]-p["Call"]["200"]["european_div"],3)} with them; the put premium falls from {f(p["Put"]["200"]["american_no_div"]-p["Put"]["200"]["european_no_div"],3)} to {f(p["Put"]["200"]["american_div"]-p["Put"]["200"]["european_div"],3)}, because a put holder wants to wait for the price drop. Dividends lowered both calls and raised both puts. Just before the second dividend, exercising the call is optimal when S is above about ${f(p["call_exercise_boundary_before_D2"],3)}. This is the price where exercising now (S − 50) equals holding a 0.1-year call on S − 1.50. That continuation value is computed with a separate 40-step CRR tree over the remaining 0.1 years and cross-checked with GBSM, which gives ${f(p["call_exercise_boundary_before_D2_gbsm"],3)} (after the last dividend an American call equals a European call).</p>')
add('<p>Gamma is the Greek that does not settle down. With N fixed, every terminal node is S₀ times a fixed factor (minus fixed dividends), and every payoff and exercise decision is a max of linear functions. So the tree value is a piecewise-linear function of S₀, with kinks wherever some node crosses the strike or the exercise boundary. A central second difference measures curvature. With a large bump it averages over many kinks and approximates the true gamma. With a tiny bump it lands either on a flat linear piece (gamma ≈ 0, as for the call at a $0.001 bump) or across a kink (a spike). Delta is a first difference of the same piecewise-linear function and stays stable. In practice, gamma from a tree should be taken from the tree nodes at step 2 or with a bump of at least one tree step.</p>')
add(f'<p>Per dollar of dividend, the first dividend moves the American call about {f(abs(sens["american_D1"])/abs(sens["american_D2"]),1)} times as much as the second (∂V/∂D₁ = {f(sens["american_D1"],3)}, ∂V/∂D₂ = {f(sens["american_D2"],3)}). Part (a) explains why. D₁ = $0.25 is below its $0.892 threshold, so exercising before it is never optimal and the holder cannot avoid it. Every dollar of D₁ lowers the stock price in every state, and the call loses roughly its delta per dollar (delta ≈ 0.50, measured sensitivity {f(abs(sens["american_D1"]),2)}). D₂ = $1.50 is far above its $0.224 threshold. In the states where the call is deep in the money (S above about ${f(p["call_exercise_boundary_before_D2"],2)}), the holder exercises just before D₂ and never bears it. D₂ only hurts the states where the call is held through the dividend, which are the states where the call is less valuable and has lower delta. For the European call, which cannot avoid either dividend, the two sensitivities are similar ({f(sens["european_D1"],3)} and {f(sens["european_D2"],3)}).</p>')
p=D['problem3'];b=p['books'];add('<h2>3. VaR of an option book</h2><h3>Predict</h3>')
add(tab(['Book','Value ($)','Delta','Gamma','Theta ($/yr)','Flat-price 10-day P&L ($)'],[[name,f(v['value'],2),f(v['delta'],2),f(v['gamma'],3),f(v['theta_annual'],2),f(v['flat_price_pnl'],2)] for name,v in b.items()]))
add('<p>Greeks suggest the short straddle and hedged short call will have the largest delta-normal understatement: both have little first-order exposure but negative gamma and positive theta. The covered call retains substantial stock delta but also negative gamma, suggesting a smaller understatement; the risk reversal has positive gamma and substantial delta, suggesting a small overstatement. I rank the likely misstatement short straddle, hedged short call, covered call, then risk reversal. All four have positive initial theta, so I expect a gain if ABC does not move for ten days. For ES/VaR, I predict the short straddle and the hedged short call will be well above 1.25: they are short gamma with almost no delta, so the P&L is close to −½Γ(ΔS)², a one-sided distribution whose losses grow with the square of the move, giving a long left tail. The covered call and risk reversal are dominated by their delta, so their P&L is close to linear in ΔS and their ES/VaR should be close to the normal 1.25, raised a little by the fat tails of ABC returns.</p>')
add('<h3>Fit</h3>')
m=p['return_moments'];fit=p['fits'];add(f'<p>From 500 arithmetic returns: mean {pct(m["mean"],3)}, sample standard deviation {pct(m["std"],3)}, skewness {f(m["skew"])}, excess kurtosis {f(m["excess_kurtosis"])}. Normal MLE μ = {f(fit["normal"]["params"][0],6)}, σ = {f(fit["normal"]["params"][1],6)}, AICc = {f(fit["normal"]["AICc"],2)}. Student t MLE df = {f(fit["student_t"]["params"][0],2)}, location = {f(fit["student_t"]["params"][1],6)}, scale = {f(fit["student_t"]["params"][2],6)}, AICc = {f(fit["student_t"]["AICc"],2)}. Lower AICc selects {"Student t" if fit["student_t"]["AICc"]<fit["normal"]["AICc"] else "normal"}. The location is set to zero for 10-day simulation as instructed; fitted scale and degrees of freedom remain fixed. Each scenario compounds ten independent daily returns and fully reprices options with 20 trading days left. There are {p["simulation_draws"]:,} scenarios per law with fixed seeds.</p>')
rows=[]
