c=p['capm'];add(f'<p>CAPM for PUTW on EQ excess monthly return: alpha {pct(c["alpha_monthly"],3)} per month, t = {f(c["alpha_t"],2)}, beta = {f(c["beta"],3)}, R² = {f(c["r2"],3)}. In the {c["downside_n"]} months when EQ excess return was below −5%, beta = {f(c["downside_beta"],3)} (SE {f(c["downside_beta_se"],3)}).</p>')
add('<h3>Reconcile</h3><p>The optimizer buys exposure to put-writing losses in exchange for a high average monthly premium. Sharpe compresses the return distribution to mean and standard deviation; it cannot distinguish a symmetric return from a negatively skewed one with the same two moments. ES averages the worst losses. Its coherence means monotonicity, cash translation, positive homogeneity, and subadditivity: losses cannot be made safer by worsening outcomes, adding cash shifts risk predictably, scaling scales risk, and diversification does not increase the sum of standalone tail risks. Those properties make portfolio tail exposure interpretable in a way that the mean–variance Sharpe ratio cannot provide. The single CAPM slope hides the state-dependent, much larger positive downside beta. Thus PUTW’s regression alpha is not evidence of skill by itself; it is partly compensation for crash insurance.</p>')
add(tab(['Asset','Bootstrap long-only weight: 5%','Median','95%'],[[n]+[pct(z,1) for z in v] for n,v in p['bootstrap_long_only_weight_quantiles'].items()]))
add('<p>Mean monthly standard errors above are material relative to expected excess returns. The bootstrap weight intervals show which optimized allocations survive sampling variability; weights that often fall to zero or vary widely are poor point estimates. A cap on PUTW is more defensible than relying on an unconstrained tangency weight inferred from this sample.</p>')
